+# Decision: Change Delivery Approach
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event, and editors submit commands over HTTPS. That half of the system is settled. What remains open is the route a committed event revision takes from the server to the browsers watching the board: how browsers learn that a stall closed, and what each tier has to do to make that happen.
+
+The pilot shape sets the load. Forty weekend markets, about 120 coordinators, up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers, 10-25 editors per busy market, two status changes per minute with bursts around opening time. Staff browse on phones and tablets around the market floor, so connectivity is intermittent by nature rather than by fault.
+
+The choice is load-bearing because it is not confined to one component. Three of the four design nodes are unwritten, and the route determines what each of them is for: whether the change store answers cursor queries or replays a revision log, whether delivery answers requests or manages long-lived sessions, whether browsers run a timer or maintain a connection they must repair. Choosing wrong means not a slower board but three nodes' worth of intent built around the wrong contract.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gate eliminated an option. All three routes meet the 15-second freshness goal, carry 800 simultaneous browsers and 20-edit-per-second bursts, and stay inside the data boundary the tenets draw — event updates are stall IDs and operational statuses, so none of the options puts customer records in a third party's hands. Those are background, not discriminators. The decision turns entirely on weighted criteria.
+
+- **Recurring operating cost at pilot scale** — *major*. Forty markets, each with its own browsing staff, is a connection count that a per-connection price multiplies. A cost that scales with browsers is paid every event weekend for as long as the board runs, and it is the kind of cost that is hard to walk back once boards depend on it.
+- **Substitutability of the delivery path** — *major*. Whether the route can be changed later without rewriting the browser and delivery nodes around someone else's model. Note that no project tenet pushes this way: *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* explicitly allows a managed route, so this criterion carries the project's own priority rather than a tenet's.
+- **Operational burden the project absorbs** — *major*, and it cuts against anything long-lived. Connection capacity planning, a reconnect path that actually works on market wifi, and a socket tier someone has to watch during the 99.5-percent availability window are real work that does not exist with request/response delivery.
+- **Behavior on flaky market connectivity** — *moderate*. Staff move around a market floor; connections drop. What matters is not whether drops happen but whether a drop is visible and recoverable, per the tenet that *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*.
+- **Update latency in normal operation** — *moderate*, not major. The binding goal is 15 seconds and subsecond updates are useful rather than required, so latency differences only count where they approach the goal or make the board feel stale to someone standing next to the stall that just closed.
+- **Load the approach places on the change store** — *minor*. At two changes per minute against 800 browsers, read amplification is a cost worth naming but not one that decides anything at pilot scale.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers ask the server for anything new every five seconds, passing the revision they last saw; the server answers with the revisions after that cursor, or with nothing. Delivery stays pure request/response, which means it inherits every property the HTTPS command path already has: the same authorization, the same load balancer, the same failure modes, nothing held open. The change store grows a cursor query — give me revisions after N for this event — and nothing else. Browsers run a timer. There is no connection to lose, so there is no reconnect logic: a browser that misses a poll simply polls again, and recovering after twenty minutes offline is the same code path as a routine poll with a stale cursor.
+
+- **Recurring operating cost**: strong. No per-connection pricing and no socket tier; cost is the request volume against infrastructure already provisioned for commands.
+- **Substitutability**: strong. Nothing about the browser or delivery contract commits to a transport beyond HTTPS; the cursor query survives any later route.
+- **Operational burden**: strong. No connection capacity to plan, no reconnect path, no long-lived sessions to watch.
+- **Flaky connectivity**: strong. A failed poll is a single failed request; the next one succeeds and carries the gap with it. Staleness is bounded by the poll interval regardless of how often the network drops.
+- **Update latency**: weak. Every change waits up to five seconds before any browser asks for it, and the lag is paid on every change rather than in exceptional cases.
+- **Change store load**: weak. 800 browsers polling every five seconds is roughly 160 reads per second against a board that changes twice a minute, so nearly every read returns nothing.
+
+**Summary**: the cheapest and least operationally demanding route, bought with a few seconds of lag on every change and reads that mostly find nothing.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds an open WebSocket per viewing browser and pushes each committed revision over it as it commits. Updates arrive as fast as the network carries them, with no vendor in the path and no per-connection bill. The change store keeps a replayable per-event revision log and publishes committed revisions to the socket tier reliably, so a revision cannot commit to the database and then fail to reach anyone. Delivery becomes a stateful tier: it authorizes at connect, tracks which connection subscribes to which event, fans out published revisions, and decides what happens when connection capacity runs out. Browsers maintain a connection and repair it — detect the drop, show it, retry with backoff, and on reconnect name the last revision they hold so the server can bring them current from the log.
+
+- **Recurring operating cost**: strong. Connections run on infrastructure the project already operates; there is no per-connection price to multiply by forty markets.
+- **Substitutability**: strong. The wire protocol and the catch-up contract are the project's own, so the route can be reshaped without renegotiating with a provider's model.
+- **Operational burden**: weak. Connection capacity becomes a planning problem, reconnect-and-catch-up becomes code that must be correct for the board to be trustworthy, and the socket tier joins the list of things that have to stay up during event hours.
+- **Flaky connectivity**: partial. Drops are frequent on a market floor and each one needs detection, backoff, and catch-up to be right. The tenet's requirement — that a failure be visible — is satisfiable and arguably better served here than by polling, since a dropped connection is a positive signal rather than an absent one, but it is satisfied by code the project writes rather than by the transport's nature.
+- **Update latency**: strong. Revisions arrive on commit; nothing waits for a timer.
+- **Change store load**: strong. One publication per committed revision, plus replay reads only when a browser reconnects.
+
+**Summary**: fast updates with no vendor and no per-connection bill, paid for in connection capacity and reconnect correctness the project owns.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A realtime provider holds the connections. The server publishes committed revisions to the provider's channels; browsers subscribe with the provider's client library. Low-latency delivery arrives largely finished — connection capacity, scaling, and reconnect are the provider's problem and their client handles the drop-and-resume case that would otherwise be ours to write. The change store still needs to reconcile what it has committed against what it has successfully published, since the publication now crosses a network boundary into a system the project cannot inspect. Delivery shrinks to a wrapper around the provider's publish API plus credential management. Browsers adopt the provider's subscription model, which means the browser-sync node's design is shaped by that library's lifecycle rather than by a contract the project chose.
+
+- **Recurring operating cost**: weak. Per-connection or per-message pricing scales with exactly the thing the pilot has a lot of — simultaneous browsers across forty markets — and recurs every event weekend.
+- **Substitutability**: weak. The provider's subscription model reaches into the browser node and its publish semantics into the change store's reconciliation; replacing it later means reopening two nodes, not swapping a library.
+- **Operational burden**: strong. Connection capacity, scaling, and reconnect are operated by the provider; what remains is credentials and publication reconciliation.
+- **Flaky connectivity**: strong. Drop-and-resume is a solved path in a mature provider's client, including the catch-up semantics that are easy to get subtly wrong by hand.
+- **Update latency**: strong. Push delivery on commit, same as self-hosted sockets.
+- **Change store load**: strong. One publication per committed revision, with reconciliation reads when publication outcomes are uncertain.
+
+**Summary**: the fastest route to low-latency delivery with the least connection operations, bought with recurring fees and a provider's model embedded in two nodes.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+The two major criteria that discriminate — recurring operating cost and substitutability of the delivery path — both separate the hosted service from the other two options, and between the remaining two, latency separates sockets from polling. Polling is the stronger option on every cost and burden criterion and loses on the one thing the board is for: a coordinator standing at a stall that just closed should not watch a board that is five seconds behind them, even though the 15-second goal forgives it. That is a deliberate preference for responsiveness over the simplest operation, not a requirement the goals impose.
+
+The decisive cost is accepted with open eyes: operational burden is a *major* criterion and this option is *weak* on it. Connection capacity planning, reconnect with backoff, and catch-up from a held revision are now the project's work, and the flaky-connectivity criterion is only *partial* here because that work has to be correct before the board can be trusted on a market floor. The judgment is that this is one-time engineering against a recurring fee and a permanent coupling, and that a transport the project owns is worth more over the life of the board than connection operations it never has to learn.
+
+This is taken against the permissiveness of the tenet that *self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both permitted* — nothing in the project forbade the hosted route; it was weighed and lost on cost and coupling. The tenets that the choice must now live up to are the other two: a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of delivery route, which is why the change store owes reliable publication rather than best-effort notification; and connectivity failures must be visible, which is why a dropped socket is a state the browser shows rather than a silence it tolerates.
+
+Committing forecloses the hosted provider's subscription model as an influence on the browser node, and forecloses treating delivery as stateless request/response. It obligates, downstream: a replayable per-event revision log and reliable publication to the socket tier in the change store; connect-time authorization, per-event subscription, fan-out, and a capacity policy in delivery; and disconnect display, reconnect with backoff, and catch-up from the last held revision in browser sync. Whether a polled HTTPS route also survives as a fallback for browsers that cannot establish a socket is a separate decision this one does not settle — it would add cursor queries to the change store and a second delivery mode to browser sync.
